<article class="env-lemma" id="00PU">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00PU">Lemma <span data-tag="00PU">115.5.1</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring and let $\varphi : R[x] \to S$ be a ring map. Let $t \in S$. If $t$ is integral over $R[x]$, then there exists an $\ell \geq 0$ such that for every $a \in R$ the element $\varphi (a)^\ell t$ is integral over $\varphi _ a : R[y] \to S$, defined by $y \mapsto \varphi (ax)$ and $r \mapsto \varphi (r)$ for $r\in R$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Say $t^ d + \sum _{i < d} \varphi (f_ i)t^ i = 0$ with $f_ i \in R[x]$. Let $\ell $ be the maximum degree in $x$ of all the $f_ i$. Multiply the equation by $\varphi (a)^\ell $ to get $\varphi (a)^\ell t^ d + \sum _{i < d} \varphi (a^\ell f_ i)t^ i = 0$. Note that each $\varphi (a^\ell f_ i)$ is in the image of $\varphi _ a$. The result follows from Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00PQ" data-tag="00PQ">10.123.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>